{
  "notes": [
    "Tile lighting effects drawn by every map view (src/maps/tileArt.js). Presentation only.",
    "glows: tile id -> light colour (a coloured light pool round the tile, plus soft contact shadows on floors next to blocks); activeGlows: tile id -> light colour while the tile is active (a live hazard).",
    "wallGlows: wall id -> light colour for lit wall fittings: a smaller light pool on the floor in front of the face the fitting is on (+y first, then +x; none when neither is open floor), which also shows through a dark map. Each colour is the average of that fitting's light layer (art-layers/), so recolouring the art means re-measuring here. The computer banks' multicoloured status lights average to a near-white. Designer request (Oct 2026); pool size and strength are implementation picks awaiting review.",
    "animations: tile id -> a flipbook of whole sprites that replaces the tile's image (designer request Oct 2026: whole alternate sprites, not overlays). frames: the image index shown in each frameTime-second step of one loop; images: one entry per index, with the sprite for each animated image of the tile (image; bigImage for its 2x2 image; panel.x / panel.y for its two-tile panel art) and its emission (emission, bigEmission, panelEmission: the lit parts alone, kept at full brightness in a dark map). active: plays only while the tile is active (a live hazard). Each tile on a map is offset in time so machines don't flash together. Written by scripts/makeTileFrames.mjs, which bakes the frames from the tile's art and its light layers (art-layers/); the frame times and the number of brightness steps are implementation picks matching the old overlay timings.",
    "wallVariants: wall id -> { share, variants: [{ id, image }] }. In the map views about share of that wall's tiles are drawn as one of the variants instead, chosen from the tile's position. Never on a panel half with a window. Empty by designer decision (Oct 2026): the bulkhead's random Star Trek wall fittings made placed walls look random, so fittings are placed by hand (lcarsWall etc.).",
    "Tile art lives in public/art/sprites/environment/<palette group id>/ (tiles.json paletteGroups), rendered at 4x resolution: 256 x 384 for a 64 x 96 tile image, taller for walls and tall objects (tiles.json imageHeight), 512 x 768 for a big object's 2x2 image. The views draw each image at its own design size, never stretched."
  ],
